Z||000005||Voytek||08-15-2001||01:49 PM||||I've gone through fixing my system about 3-4 weeks ago.  To convert to R-134 they tell you (and you'll see this in Ogre's Cave too) that all you need to do is change ALL the fittings, drain the system and re-charge it.  I've been told by a few shops that this is NOT the complete conversion.  A proper conversion should also see all the rubber hoses changed, in part because R-134 operates under some really high pressures (at least 200 psi, I believe).<P>As far as the compressor, receiver/drier (silver can) and orifice tube - NO, you DO NOT need to change any of those UNLESS they ar faulty in any way (or plugged in the case of the orifice tube).<BR>IF, however, your compressor IS faulty, THEN you DO need to change the received/drier (it has old oil accummulated in it and you need to change it if you don't want to void your compressor warranty) AND they do recommend changing the orifice tube just in case the compressor seized.  If it seized, there is a chance that bits of its internals went down the hoses and got stopped at the orifice tube (hopefully).  If your compressor clutch simply does not engage or is leaky, there's no need to change the tube.<P>Do a search - I posted a topic on converting A/C recently to a new refrigerant called Duracool.  You would have to check if it's legal in California (it is legal here in Canada).  The bonus is that you only need to change 2 fittings on the receiver/drier (about $10 CDN) and you're ready for a refill.  This stuff is compatible with the rest of the system.  My system operates at 50-90 psi (depending on how hot it is), compared to around 200 psi for R-134.  I drove to Nevada 2 weeks ago - kept me nice and cold all the way there and back.<P>Also, are you converting because you want to or because your system is dead?  If it IS dead, I would take it to a shop first and do a leak test - you may save yourself some money and replace only the parts you need to.  A compressor runs about $200-$250 US.  A receiver drier cost me $70 CDN (about $48 US) and the refill with Duracool cost me $180 CDN -about $120 US (including vacuum, leak dye and the 2 fittings).  An R-134 recharge will run you about the same but you will also need to replace the fittings.  There's a kit you can buy at Napa for around $40 US I believe (it's $65 bucks here).  I don't know how much the hoses are IF you'll decide to replace them.<BR>If you need the compressor, there are two kinds out there.  The Fiero will use the SR compressor (I think that's the number - but definitely NOT the DA6 - I've been through this with several parts stores.)  The DA6 is an older style.  I would also recommend replacing the low pressure and high pressure switches on the back of the compressor.  I've replaced only one (high side) due to lack of time (the low side was not in town) for about $20 CDN (that's about $13 USD).  I wouldn't say this is a must but if you're going to replace the compressor, this is not a bad investment.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Voytek||08-15-2001||01:52 PM||||One more thing.  If you ARE replacing the compressor, let me know.  There is a couple of things you need to do that not every shop will tell you.||||reg||1
